When we call `makeSound` with a new instance of `Duck`, TypeScript knows that the type of the argument passed to `makeSound` is compatible with the `Bird` interface, even though we don't define `Duck` as implementing the `fly()` method. This is because TypeScript uses structural typing, which means that types are based on their shape (i.e., the set of properties and methods they have) rather than their names.

In this sense, TypeScript allows for Duck Typing by allowing structural compatibility between types, while also providing optional type annotations and interfaces to increase type safety and expressiveness.

